<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  ===========================================================================
  THE CART PAGE (Figma 817:22551 / 553:4663 desktop, 669:13034 mobile)
  ===========================================================================
  The two desktop cart frames are near-identical; per the implementation
  contract this builds ONE authoritative structure rather than two.

  ===========================================================================
  TEMPLATES ARE NOT DECLARED HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE
  ===========================================================================
  A template placed in the theme at the path core declares -
  Magento_Checkout::cart/form.phtml, Magento_Checkout::cart/item/default.phtml -
  is picked up by theme fallback for EVERY block that references it. Writing
  <action method="setTemplate"> for the same path would be a second, weaker way
  of saying the same thing: weaker because it names blocks one at a time, and
  core declares the item template on eight renderers (default, simple, virtual,
  configurable, grouped, bundle, downloadable...) - so a hand-written list
  silently misses whichever one it forgot, and that product type renders core's
  table markup inside a card layout.

  The quantity view model reaches those renderers by DI for the same reason -
  see Spartrak_Checkout/etc/frontend/di.xml.

  ===========================================================================
  WHAT THIS FILE ACTUALLY DOES: REGROUPS THE SUMMARY COLUMN
  ===========================================================================
  Figma's summary column is two stacked panels inside one container:

      Order summary container      516 wide
        Order summary details      grey #f9f9f9, 8px padding, 8px gap
          Shipping details         the "الشحن الي" row
          Order summary card       white, 24px padding - title, totals, CTA
        Gift Option Container      the instalments panel

  Core emits title, shipping, totals and the checkout button as four FLAT
  siblings of cart.summary. CSS alone cannot draw a box around three of four
  siblings, so two containers are introduced and core's own blocks are moved
  into them. Nothing is replaced or removed - every block still renders, in the
  same order, with its own template and behaviour.

  ===========================================================================
  WHAT IS KEPT FROM CORE, AND WHY THAT MATTERS
  ===========================================================================
    checkout.cart.form      still posts to checkout/cart/updatePost, so
                            quantity changes, removals and clear-cart all run
                            through core's validated controller with its CSRF
                            form key. Nothing here invents a write path.
    checkout.cart.totals    still renders core's Knockout totals components, so
                            tax display, discounts and any third-party total
                            behave exactly as configured. The table they emit
                            is restyled in LESS rather than replaced - see
                            _cart.less for why keeping it is the safer choice.
    checkout.cart.shipping  core's shipping estimator. Figma restyles it into
                            the collapsible ship-to row; the component and its
                            rate lookup are untouched.
    checkout.cart.coupon    untouched and left where Porto puts it. Figma draws
                            no promo field on the CART (it is on the payment
                            step), so the block is not removed - a merchant or
                            extension may still surface it.

  The mega-menu row STAYS. The cart navbar is 223.42 tall in Figma against the
  checkout's 157.42, and the difference is exactly the menu row - so, unlike
  checkout_index_index.xml, this file does not remove spartrak.primary_nav.
-->
<page layout="1column" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
      xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:View/Layout/etc/page_configuration.xsd">
    <!--
      The cart's stylesheet, loaded here and nowhere else. It is NOT part of
      spartrak-components.css on purpose: that bundle is render-blocking on
      every page, and this file matches nothing outside this handle. See
      web/css/spartrak-cart.less.
    -->
    <head>
        <css src="css/spartrak-cart.css"/>
    </head>
    <body>
        <!--
          THE BREADCRUMB. 553:4760 opens on the cart header — there is no trail
          above it, and no `سلة التسوق` link back to the page the shopper is
          already on. Removed rather than hidden: it is a block with a template
          and a JS init, and none of it has anywhere to go from here.
        -->
        <referenceBlock name="breadcrumbs" remove="true"/>

        <!--
          THE PAGE TITLE. Core prints `سلة التسوق` in an <h1> above the columns.
          Figma's cart opens on `عربة التسوق لديك` (553:4806), which the cart
          FORM already renders as this page's <h1> — see
          Magento_Checkout::cart/form.phtml.

          So the two were not a design difference, they were two <h1> elements
          on one document: a duplicate heading, a second landmark in the
          accessibility tree, and a stack of Porto page-title margins above a
          header the design draws flush. Core's is the one that goes, because
          the surviving heading has to sit inside the cart column where Figma
          puts it, not in a full-width band above both columns.
        -->
        <referenceBlock name="page.main.title" remove="true"/>

        <referenceContainer name="cart.summary">
            <!-- The grey ground that holds the ship-to row and the white card. -->
            <container name="spartrak.cart.summary.panel"
                       htmlTag="div"
                       htmlClass="spartrak-cart-summary"
                       before="-"/>

            <!-- The instalments panel sits OUTSIDE that ground, below it. -->
            <block class="Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template"
                   name="spartrak.cart.instalments"
                   after="spartrak.cart.summary.panel"
                   template="Magento_Checkout::cart/instalments.phtml">
                <arguments>
                    <argument name="instalments_view_model" xsi:type="object">
                        Spartrak\Checkout\ViewModel\InstalmentsPromo
                    </argument>
                </arguments>
            </block>
        </referenceContainer>

        <!--
          The `الشحن الي` row needs the customer's default shipping address, and
          the theme's cart/shipping.phtml reads it from here. An argument rather
          than a helper call in the template, so the lookup and its failure
          handling live in a class (CLAUDE.md §8) — see ViewModel\CartShipTo.
        -->
        <referenceBlock name="checkout.cart.shipping">
            <arguments>
                <argument name="ship_to_view_model" xsi:type="object">
                    Spartrak\Checkout\ViewModel\CartShipTo
                </argument>
            </arguments>
        </referenceBlock>

        <referenceContainer name="spartrak.cart.summary.panel">
            <!-- The white card: title, totals and the checkout CTA. -->
            <container name="spartrak.cart.summary.card"
                       htmlTag="div"
                       htmlClass="spartrak-cart-summary__card"
                       after="checkout.cart.shipping"/>
        </referenceContainer>

        <!--
          ===================================================================
          POSITIONS ARE ABSOLUTE (`-`), NOT RELATIVE TO A SIBLING
          ===================================================================
          The ship-to row first, then the card - the order Figma stacks them in.
          Moved rather than re-declared so each block keeps the arguments,
          jsLayout and child blocks core gave it.

          Every one of these used to name a SIBLING - `after="checkout.cart
          .summary.title"` and so on - and on the rendered page the column came
          out in the wrong order: the totals and the CTA landed in the white
          card, but the title fell to the BOTTOM of it and the estimator sat
          below the card instead of above it.

          The cause is that a relative position can only be honoured if the
          element it names is already a child of the destination at the moment
          the move is processed, and these moves are what PUT it there. Magento
          resolves what it can and appends the rest, which is exactly the order
          that showed up.

          `before="-"` and `after="-"` mean first and last outright. They depend
          on nothing, so the order is the same whatever sequence Magento
          processes the moves in.
        -->
        <move element="checkout.cart.shipping" destination="spartrak.cart.summary.panel" before="-"/>
        <move element="checkout.cart.summary.title" destination="spartrak.cart.summary.card" before="-"/>
        <move element="checkout.cart.totals.container" destination="spartrak.cart.summary.card"
              after="checkout.cart.summary.title"/>
        <move element="checkout.cart.methods.bottom" destination="spartrak.cart.summary.card" after="-"/>

        <!--
          ===================================================================
          THREE CONTROLS FIGMA DOES NOT DRAW, REMOVED RATHER THAN HIDDEN
          ===================================================================
          Each one is a block with a template, and in two cases a child tree.
          `display: none` would still build and render all of it and leave it in
          the accessibility tree; `remove="true"` stops the layout before it is
          instantiated.

          THE COUPON FIELD. Figma's cart has no promo input - 553:4762 holds the
          ship-to row, the totals card and the instalments panel and nothing
          else. The coupon belongs to the CHECKOUT's summary column
          (554:11386), where Spartrak_Checkout mounts it. Two places to enter
          one code, one of which the design never drew, is a second answer to a
          question the funnel already answers.

          THE MULTI-ADDRESS LINK. `الدفع لعناوين متعددة`, rendered by
          Magento_Multishipping into the CTA block. Figma draws one button under
          the totals. Multi-address checkout is also declined by this store's
          own payment method (see Spartrak_InstaPay's payment.xml), so the link
          led somewhere InstaPay cannot follow.

          MOVE TO WISHLIST. `نقل إلى المفضلة`, printed beside the bin on every
          line. Figma's cart line (820:16560) has exactly two controls: the
          remove button and the quantity. One entry per product TYPE, because
          Magento_Wishlist declares the block once per renderer - a `remove` on
          a name that does not exist is a no-op, so listing all of them is safe
          and none can be missed.
        -->
        <referenceBlock name="checkout.cart.coupon" remove="true"/>
        <referenceBlock name="checkout.cart.methods.multishipping" remove="true"/>

        <referenceBlock name="checkout.cart.item.renderers.default.actions.move_to_wishlist" remove="true"/>
        <referenceBlock name="checkout.cart.item.renderers.simple.actions.move_to_wishlist" remove="true"/>
        <referenceBlock name="checkout.cart.item.renderers.virtual.actions.move_to_wishlist" remove="true"/>
        <referenceBlock name="checkout.cart.item.renderers.grouped.actions.move_to_wishlist" remove="true"/>
        <referenceBlock name="checkout.cart.item.renderers.bundle.actions.move_to_wishlist" remove="true"/>
        <referenceBlock name="checkout.cart.item.renderers.downloadable.actions.move_to_wishlist" remove="true"/>
        <referenceBlock name="checkout.cart.item.renderers.configurable.actions.move_to_wishlist" remove="true"/>
    </body>
</page>
